  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 826 PM MDT Wed Apr 22 2026\n\nTomorrow brings a moderate increase in winds as a broad upper low \nmoves through the west coast trough, well to our north. This upper \nlow will stay way north, but it's movement off the west coast toward \nthe Northern Rockies will squeeze our pressure gradients, and deepen \nthe lee-side surface trough to our east. This will turn our winds \nwesterly, and increase them up from breezy into the windy category. \nWe will remain critically dry, with very low RH, and just some \npassing high and mid level clouds.\nWest winds of 20 to 25 mph with gusts 30-40 mph, and single digit \nrelative humidity. Not windy enough for any wind headlines, but we \ndo have fire headlines. Some blowing dust is possible, with patchy \nblowing dust across the southern lowlands. Do not expect widespread \nsignificant visibility reductions, but it could happen over local \nareas near dust sources.\n\nFriday is a \"tweener\" day, and so is Saturday for the most part. \nWind will be lighter Friday, and back down to marginally breezy \nterritory. For Saturday, the afternoon will start to see the effects \nof the next Pacific storm system well to our west, and winds will be \nback on the increase. Temperatures will be a few degrees above \naverage, conditions will be dry.\n\nSunday should deliver our most impactful day of the forecast, as the \nPacific trough opens up and lifts across AZ, and we see a \nsignificant increase in southwest and west winds across the entire \nregion. The jet associated with the broad trough will be directly \noverhead the we will see a deep surface low form over NE New Mexico. \nThese strong winds aloft and sharp pressure gradients near the \nsurface will make for windy conditions. Winds in the 20-30 mph \nrange, with gusts 50-60 are expected. Again, fire weather concerns \nwill be high with critical conditions expected. Blowing dust will \nlikely be the most impactful across the region, with areas of \nsignificant visibility reductions.\n\n...NEXT WEEK... We get another short break in strong winds for next \nMonday, as a minor shortwave ridge builds behind Sunday's exiting \ntrough. By Tuesday, we are back to breezy conditions with more \ntroughing developing to our west. Wednesday a fetch of subtropical \nmoisture looks to move in over our western areas ahead on the west \ncoast trough. We could see low-end precipitation chances midweek.\nHowever, the rain outlook looks brief and light.",
